= Adaptation (systems) =

== Overview ==
In systems science, '''adaptation''' is change in a system's state, structure, policy, organization, or behavior in response to internal or environmental conditions such that persistence, fit, or performance is maintained or improved relative to some criterion.

Adaptation appears in biological, computational, engineered, ecological, and social systems.

== Mechanisms ==
Systems can adapt through:

* [[Feedback]];
* [[Learning]];
* parameter adjustment;
* structural reconfiguration;
* behavioral change;
* evolutionary processes;
* policy or strategy change;
* network rewiring.

No one mechanism defines all adaptation.

== Adaptation and evolution ==
Biological evolution can produce adaptation across generations.

But systems adaptation also includes within-lifetime learning, physiological regulation, controller adjustment, and organizational change.

Therefore:

<pre>
adaptation ≠ evolution by definition
</pre>

== Adaptation and robustness ==
[[Robustness]] preserves function despite perturbation.

Adaptation changes the system in response to conditions.

A robust system may require little change; an adaptive system may reconfigure to remain effective.

The two can complement each other.

== Adaptation and optimization ==
Some adaptive systems can be modeled as optimizing an objective.

Others change through local rules, selection, feedback, or path-dependent dynamics without solving one explicit global optimization problem.

== Complex adaptive systems ==
A complex adaptive system contains interacting components whose behavior or structure changes in response to experience/environment, potentially altering the environment for other components.

Adaptation can therefore be coevolutionary rather than one-way adjustment to a fixed environment.

